<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util"
     xmlns:fw="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/firewall">

  <!--
    The SEL LIVE Windows Agent installer (§44).

    ── Why MSI and not MSIX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    MSIX needs Windows 10 1809 or later, which would exclude every Windows 7 and 8.1 machine in
    the estate — the whole reason this agent targets .NET Framework. Windows Installer has been
    present since Windows 2000 and installs the same package on every supported release, and it
    is the only packaging format that can install a Windows service, which MSIX cannot do at all.

    ── Why WiX 5 produces a package that installs on Windows 7 ───────────────────────────────────

    The toolset is modern; the output is not. InstallerVersion is set to 405 (Windows Installer
    4.5), which shipped with Windows 7 and is present on every later release. Nothing here uses a
    table or an action introduced after that, so the MSI is readable by the installer engine on a
    2009 machine even though it was authored with a 2024 toolchain. Only the *build* machine needs
    anything current.
  -->

  <Package Name="SEL LIVE Windows Agent"
           Manufacturer="Siddhartha Engineering Limited"
           Version="1.2.0.0"
           UpgradeCode="7E2D9A34-4C6B-4E3B-9A1D-2C7F8B5E6D41"
           Language="1033"
           Scope="perMachine"
           Compressed="yes"
           InstallerVersion="405">

    <SummaryInformation Description="SEL LIVE work activity, attendance and notification agent" />

    <!-- Upgrades replace in place; a downgrade is refused with a readable message rather than
         half-installing over a newer build. -->
    <MajorUpgrade
      AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
      DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of the SEL LIVE Agent is already installed. Uninstall it first if you genuinely need to go back." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- ══ Prerequisites ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Checked before a single file is copied. Each one has a message that says what to do,
         because "Setup failed" on a site-office PC turns into a phone call.
    -->

    <!-- .NET Framework 4.8. WixNetFxExtension's property is the supported way to detect it;
         the raw registry read is kept as the condition so the package has no extension
         dependency for something this simple. -->
    <Property Id="NETFRAMEWORK48RELEASE" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="NetFx48Release"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\NET Framework Setup\NDP\v4\Full"
                      Name="Release"
                      Type="raw" />
    </Property>

    <Launch Condition="Installed OR NETFRAMEWORK48RELEASE &gt;= &quot;#528040&quot;"
            Message="The SEL LIVE Agent needs Microsoft .NET Framework 4.8. Install it (it is a free download from Microsoft, and is already present on Windows 10 1903 and later) and run this installer again." />

    <!-- Windows 7 SP1 is 6.1.7601. VersionNT is 601 for Windows 7, 603 for 8.1, 1000 for 10/11.
         602 (Windows 8.0) is excluded on purpose: .NET Framework 4.8 does not support it. -->
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR (VersionNT &gt;= 601 AND VersionNT &lt;&gt; 602)"
            Message="The SEL LIVE Agent supports Windows 7 SP1, Windows 8.1, Windows 10 and Windows 11. Windows 8.0 cannot run .NET Framework 4.8 — update this PC to Windows 8.1 (a free update) and run the installer again." />

    <Launch Condition="Installed OR (VersionNT &gt; 601 OR ServicePackLevel &gt;= 1)"
            Message="Windows 7 needs Service Pack 1 before the SEL LIVE Agent can be installed." />

    <!--
      There is deliberately NO `Launch Condition="Privileged"` here, and removing it is what
      makes "only an administrator can install this" actually work.

      The condition looks like the way to require admin. It is not. `Privileged` is evaluated by
      the LaunchConditions action in the *UI* sequence, which runs before Windows Installer
      elevates anything — so for a standard user it is 0, the condition fails, and they get a
      dead-end message box. They are never offered the chance to enter administrator
      credentials, and the install simply cannot proceed on the account that would normally ask
      somebody to type a password.

      Without it, the documented flow happens instead: the package is Scope="perMachine", so
      Windows Installer requests elevation at the InstallExecuteSequence boundary. An
      administrator sees a UAC consent prompt; a standard user sees a credential prompt and can
      hand the keyboard to IT. Either way the install is refused unless somebody with
      administrator rights approves it — enforced by Windows, which is both stricter and more
      helpful than a property check we wrote ourselves.
    -->

    <!-- ══ Configuration, supplied on the command line ════════════════════════════════════════
         msiexec /i SEL.Agent.msi /qn ENROLLMENTCODE=SEL-HO-2026

         APIBASEURL defaults to the company's own server and FIREBASEAPIKEY is fetched from it,
         so an ordinary rollout passes neither. Both remain settable for a staging server:

         msiexec /i SEL.Agent.msi /qn ^
           APIBASEURL=https://staging.example.com ^
           ENROLLMENTCODE=SEL-HO-2026

         Secure="yes" is what allows these to survive the elevation boundary into the deferred
         custom action. Without it they are silently empty in the elevated context and the agent
         installs with no configuration — which looks like the installer worked.
    -->
    <!--
      Defaulted to SEL LIVE's own address, so a double-click install is configured without
      anybody typing anything. Overridable on the command line for a staging server. The same
      judgement as the hard-coded Firebase project in src/lib/firebase-public-config.ts: there
      is one server this agent is built to talk to, and making four hundred PCs each be told
      about it by hand only ever produced typos.
    -->
    <Property Id="APIBASEURL" Secure="yes" Value="https://seltech.store" />
    <Property Id="FIREBASEAPIKEY" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="ENROLLMENTCODE" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="DEVICENAME" Secure="yes" />

    <!--
      Both are OPTIONAL, and that is a deliberate reversal.

      They used to be Launch conditions, which made the MSI impossible to install by
      double-clicking it: WixUI_Minimal offers nowhere to type a property, so the install could
      only ever fail with "APIBASEURL is required". An installer that can only be run from a
      command line is not a professional installer — it is a script with a .msi extension.

      Supplied, they are written straight into agent.config.json and the agent starts silently:
      that is the path for GPO, SCCM and any unattended rollout, and it stays the recommended one.

      Omitted, the install still succeeds and the agent shows its first-run setup window, which
      asks for the ERP address and fetches the Firebase key from that server's
      /api/windows-agent/bootstrap. FIREBASEAPIKEY in particular is worth not demanding: it is a
      public value, and making somebody transcribe it onto every PC only ever added a typo that
      surfaces later as an opaque Google error.
    -->

    <!-- ══ Identity ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!--
      The product icon: shortcuts, and the entry in Programs and Features.
      Taken from the agent's source tree rather than its build output, because .ico files are
      not copied to bin and an installer should not depend on one having been.
    -->
    <Icon Id="AgentIcon" SourceFile="..\SEL.Agent\app.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="AgentIcon" />

    <!--
      Whether to put a shortcut on the desktop. 1 by default, because the agent is the way into
      SEL LIVE on these machines and somebody who has just installed it should not have to go
      looking. Suppressed with DESKTOPSHORTCUT=0, and offered as a checkbox by the setup
      program so it is a choice rather than an imposition.
    -->
    <Property Id="DESKTOPSHORTCUT" Secure="yes" Value="1" />

    <!-- ══ Layout ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <StandardDirectory Id="DesktopFolder" />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFilesFolder">
      <Directory Id="ManufacturerFolder" Name="SEL LIVE">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Agent" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ShortcutFolder" Name="SEL LIVE" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- The agent's working directory: device credential, offline queue, configuration. Created
         here so its ACL is applied by the installer, which is elevated, rather than by the agent,
         which is not. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="DataManufacturerFolder" Name="SEL LIVE">
        <Directory Id="AGENTDATAFOLDER" Name="Agent" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="SEL LIVE Agent" Level="1" AllowAbsent="no">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="AgentBinaries" />
      <ComponentRef Id="AgentService" />
      <ComponentRef Id="StartMenuShortcut" />
      <ComponentRef Id="DesktopShortcut" />
      <ComponentRef Id="AgentDataFolder" />
      <ComponentRef Id="Tls12Enablement" />
    </Feature>

    <!-- ══ Components ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <ComponentGroup Id="AgentBinaries" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <!--
        Everything in the staged output, harvested rather than listed. A new dependency (a NuGet
        package picking up a transitive reference, say) then ships without anybody remembering to
        add a Component for it — which is the usual way an installer comes to be missing one DLL.

        The service executable is deliberately NOT in this bindpath. ServiceInstall takes its
        image path from its parent component's KeyPath file, so the service binary has to be an
        explicit File in the AgentService component below. Harvesting it here as well would
        produce two File rows for one path — a short-filename collision that Windows Installer
        resolves unpredictably. Keeping it in a second staging directory (bindpath `svc`) makes
        the separation structural rather than something an Exclude pattern has to get right.
      -->
      <Files Include="!(bindpath.bin)\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <Component Id="AgentService" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="B2F41C7E-9A55-4E62-8F3B-1D6A4C8E7052">
      <File Id="ServiceExe" Source="!(bindpath.svc)\SEL.Agent.Service.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      <File Id="ServiceExeConfig" Source="!(bindpath.svc)\SEL.Agent.Service.exe.config" />

      <!--
        LocalSystem, and it genuinely needs to be.

        WTSQueryUserToken — how the service starts the desktop agent inside a signed-in user's
        session — requires SE_TCB_NAME, which only LocalSystem holds. A LocalService or
        NetworkService account cannot do it, so the agent would never appear for anybody.

        Start="auto" with a delayed start: the agent has nothing useful to do until the network
        stack and the user session are up, and a non-delayed service competing with the shell at
        boot is a measurable few seconds off every morning on older hardware.
      -->
      <ServiceInstall Id="InstallAgentService"
                      Name="SELLiveAgent"
                      DisplayName="SEL LIVE Agent"
                      Description="Keeps the SEL LIVE work activity agent running for each signed-in user, and maintains its local activity queue."
                      Type="ownProcess"
                      Start="auto"
                      Account="LocalSystem"
                      ErrorControl="normal"
                      Vital="yes">
        <!-- Restart on failure rather than leaving a machine untracked. The reset period means
             a service that fails repeatedly over a day still gets retried the next day. -->
        <util:ServiceConfig FirstFailureActionType="restart"
                            SecondFailureActionType="restart"
                            ThirdFailureActionType="restart"
                            RestartServiceDelayInSeconds="60"
                            ResetPeriodInDays="1" />
      </ServiceInstall>

      <ServiceControl Id="ControlAgentService"
                      Name="SELLiveAgent"
                      Start="install"
                      Stop="both"
                      Remove="uninstall"
                      Wait="yes" />

      <!-- The event source the service logs to. Creating it needs elevation, which is why it is
           done here rather than on first run — see SelAgentService.Log. -->
      <util:EventSource Log="Application"
                        Name="SELLiveAgent"
                        EventMessageFile="%SystemRoot%\System32\EventCreate.exe" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      ══ How the agent starts, and why there is no Run key any more ═══════════════════════════

      There used to be a per-machine `...\CurrentVersion\Run` entry here, alongside the service.
      It had to go, for two reasons that turned out to be the same reason.

      First, a Run entry is listed in Task Manager's "Startup apps" tab and in Settings → Apps →
      Startup, each with a switch beside it. On a PC whose user is a local administrator — most
      of this fleet — anybody could turn the agent off before it ever ran, and nothing about the
      machine afterwards indicated that something was missing. It simply recorded nothing.

      Second, Explorer defers Run entries until the desktop is built and then adds a delay of its
      own. That is ten to thirty seconds every morning during which somebody is already working
      and nothing is watching — which was the other half of "why does the agent take so long to
      appear?".

      Both are answered by a scheduled task with a logon trigger and no delay, registered by
      the service executable’s install-logon-task switch. It is not listed in either startup UI, cannot
      be disabled without administrative rights, and runs as the session starts. See LogonTask
      for the full reasoning, including why it is deliberately not hidden from Task Scheduler.

      The service watchdog remains, and still matters: it is what brings the agent back when
      somebody ends it from Task Manager, now within thirty seconds rather than a minute.
    -->

    <!--
      The Start Menu shortcut is not cosmetic.

      A Win32 application can only be *called back* when an Action Center toast is clicked if it
      has a shortcut carrying an Application User Model ID. Without this component, Windows 10 and
      11 machines fall back to the SEL LIVE popup — which works, but loses Action Center
      persistence for no reason. ModernNotificationPresenter probes for exactly this.
    -->
    <Component Id="StartMenuShortcut" Directory="ShortcutFolder" Guid="D8B3F250-71C4-4A9E-B6D2-5E8A1C4F9037">
      <Shortcut Id="AgentShortcut"
                Name="SEL LIVE Agent"
                Description="SEL LIVE work activity and notification agent"
                Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]SEL.Agent.exe"
                WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                Icon="AgentIcon">
        <ShortcutProperty Key="System.AppUserModel.ID" Value="SiddharthaEngineering.SelLive.Agent" />
      </Shortcut>
      <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveShortcutFolder" Directory="ShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\Siddhartha Engineering\SEL LIVE Agent"
                     Name="ShortcutInstalled"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1"
                     KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      The desktop shortcut, when it was asked for.
      Its own component so the condition governs it alone: putting the condition on the Start
      Menu component would take the toast AppUserModelID registration with it, and on Windows
      10/11 that is what makes native notifications work at all.
    -->
    <Component Id="DesktopShortcut" Directory="DesktopFolder" Guid="A1F7D63B-24E8-4C50-9B71-6E3A8D2C5F94"
               Condition="DESKTOPSHORTCUT AND DESKTOPSHORTCUT &lt;&gt; &quot;0&quot;">
      <Shortcut Id="AgentDesktopShortcut"
                Name="SEL LIVE Agent"
                Description="SEL LIVE work activity and notification agent"
                Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]SEL.Agent.exe"
                WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                Icon="AgentIcon">
        <ShortcutProperty Key="System.AppUserModel.ID" Value="SiddharthaEngineering.SelLive.Agent" />
      </Shortcut>
      <!--
        Per-machine, so the shortcut appears for everybody who signs in to a shared PC — which
        is the same reasoning as the Start Menu entry. HKLM rather than HKCU for the KeyPath for
        the same reason: a per-user key would make the component "not installed" for the second
        user and Windows Installer would repair it on every launch.
      -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\Siddhartha Engineering\SEL LIVE Agent"
                     Name="DesktopShortcutInstalled"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1"
                     KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <Component Id="AgentDataFolder" Directory="AGENTDATAFOLDER" Guid="E5C92A18-3D7B-4F06-9C81-A2B4D6E8F350">
      <CreateFolder>
        <!--
          SYSTEM and Administrators full control; Users Modify. Inheritance broken.

          The Users entry is load-bearing, not a relaxation. The desktop agent runs as the
          signed-in standard user and must read device.json, read and write queue.db, and write
          its log — all of which live here. An Administrators-only ACL produces an agent that
          installs perfectly and then cannot read its own credential or record a single span.

          Leaving Users out would also have bought very little: the agent runs as that user, so
          they can read the decrypted secret out of its memory regardless of file permissions,
          and device.json is a *machine* credential that every user of a shared PC legitimately
          uses anyway. The per-person secret is the Firebase refresh token, and that lives in
          %LOCALAPPDATA%, isolated by Windows per profile.

          What still protects the credential is DPAPI with purpose entropy: a stolen disk or a
          copied backup yields ciphertext that will not decrypt on another machine. Breaking
          inheritance is still worth doing — it keeps the permissive ProgramData default from
          flowing in — and Modify rather than FullControl stops a standard user rewriting this
          ACL. See DeviceIdentityStore.TryHardenAcl for the same reasoning in code.

          0x1301BF is the standard Modify mask.
        -->
        <PermissionEx Sddl="O:BAG:BAD:PAI(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;FA;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1301BF;;;BU)" />
      </CreateFolder>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\Shyama Electrical\SEL LIVE Agent"
                     Name="DataFolder"
                     Type="string"
                     Value="[AGENTDATAFOLDER]"
                     KeyPath="yes" />
      <!-- The credential, queue and log are created at runtime, so they are removed explicitly
           rather than tracked as installed files. -->
      <RemoveFile Id="RemoveDeviceJson" Name="device.json" On="uninstall" />
      <RemoveFile Id="RemoveQueueDb" Name="queue.db*" On="uninstall" />
      <RemoveFile Id="RemoveAgentConfig" Name="agent.config.json" On="uninstall" />
      <RemoveFile Id="RemoveAgentLog" Name="agent.log*" On="uninstall" />
      <RemoveFile Id="RemoveServiceLog" Name="service.log" On="uninstall" />
      <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveAgentDataFolder" On="uninstall" />
    </Component>

    <!--
      TLS 1.2 for .NET on Windows 7.

      Written unconditionally — the values are the default on Windows 8.1 and later, so setting
      them there changes nothing, and conditioning the component would mean maintaining a version
      test in two places. What this does *not* do is enable TLS 1.2 in SChannel itself: that needs
      KB3140245 and a reboot, which an installer should not be doing silently. The prerequisite
      check (SEL.Agent.Service.exe with the check switch) reports it, and the deployment guide
      covers it.
    -->
    <Component Id="Tls12Enablement" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="F7D14B6C-8E23-4950-BA7F-C1D5E9028463">
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\.NETFramework\v4.0.30319"
                     Name="SchUseStrongCrypto"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1"
                     KeyPath="yes" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\.NETFramework\v4.0.30319"
                     Name="SystemDefaultTlsVersions"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1" />
      <!-- The WOW6432Node counterpart matters: the agent is a 32-bit process, and on x64 Windows
           it reads its .NET configuration from there. Setting only the 64-bit key is the classic
           half-fix that leaves a 32-bit application still negotiating TLS 1.0. -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\.NETFramework\v4.0.30319"
                     Name="SchUseStrongCrypto"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\.NETFramework\v4.0.30319"
                     Name="SystemDefaultTlsVersions"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1" />
    </Component>

    <!-- ══ Custom actions ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The configuration file is written by the agent's own code rather than by the installer,
         so there is one implementation of "what a valid config looks like" and it is the one
         that gets tested. See Program.WriteConfiguration for why JSON in WiX was avoided.
    -->

    <SetProperty Id="WriteAgentConfig"
                 Value="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]SEL.Agent.Service.exe&quot; --write-config --url &quot;[APIBASEURL]&quot; --key &quot;[FIREBASEAPIKEY]&quot; --code &quot;[ENROLLMENTCODE]&quot; --device-name &quot;[DEVICENAME]&quot;"
                 Before="WriteAgentConfig"
                 Sequence="execute" />

    <CustomAction Id="WriteAgentConfig"
                  BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X86"
                  DllEntry="WixQuietExec"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="check" />

    <!--
      The logon task. See the note above the components for why this replaced the Run key.

      Return="ignore" on both, deliberately. A machine with Task Scheduler locked down by policy
      should still end up with a working install: the service watchdog starts the agent within
      thirty seconds of sign-in either way, and the check switch reports the task as MISSING so the
      difference is visible rather than silent. Failing the install over it would turn a slower
      start into no agent at all.
    -->
    <SetProperty Id="InstallLogonTask"
                 Value="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]SEL.Agent.Service.exe&quot; --install-logon-task"
                 Before="InstallLogonTask"
                 Sequence="execute" />

    <CustomAction Id="InstallLogonTask"
                  BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X86"
                  DllEntry="WixQuietExec"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <SetProperty Id="RemoveLogonTask"
                 Value="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]SEL.Agent.Service.exe&quot; --remove-logon-task"
                 Before="RemoveLogonTask"
                 Sequence="execute" />

    <CustomAction Id="RemoveLogonTask"
                  BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X86"
                  DllEntry="WixQuietExec"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <!--
      ══ Approval to remove the agent ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      An employee could close the agent only with a SEL LIVE administrator's approval, and then
      uninstall it from Apps and Features with two clicks and no approval at all. This closes
      that, with the same permission the tray's Exit uses.

      The agent, run with the authorize-uninstall switch, shows the approval window and exits 0 for approved,
      1 for refused; Return="check" turns a refusal into a stopped uninstall. The agent handles
      that switch before its single-instance mutex, which is load-bearing: the agent is normally
      already running, so a second copy reaching the mutex would exit 0 and be read as approval.

      Immediate rather than deferred, and impersonating, because it has to draw a window on the
      desktop of whoever is uninstalling. That also fixes what it can protect: an interactive
      removal. It is not a security boundary — a local administrator can stop the service and
      delete the folder, and §7 says so — and a silent `/quiet` removal is exempt by the
      condition below, because blocking on a window nobody can see would leave a fleet that
      cannot be serviced remotely.
    -->
    <SetProperty Id="AuthorizeUninstall"
                 Value="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]SEL.Agent.exe&quot; --authorize-uninstall"
                 Before="AuthorizeUninstall"
                 Sequence="execute" />

    <CustomAction Id="AuthorizeUninstall"
                  BinaryRef="Wix4UtilCA_X86"
                  DllEntry="WixQuietExec"
                  Execute="immediate"
                  Impersonate="yes"
                  Return="check" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!--
        After InstallFiles so the executable exists, and before StartServices so the service
        finds a configuration on its first start rather than logging "not configured".

        Conditioned on APIBASEURL alone, which now has a default, so this runs on every fresh
        install. FIREBASEAPIKEY is no longer required: the write-config switch fetches the key
        from the server's /api/windows-agent/bootstrap when it is not given, which is both one
        fewer thing to type and one fewer copy of a value that has already gone stale once.

        Should the address be blanked deliberately (APIBASEURL=""), this is skipped and the
        agent runs its first-run setup window instead — still a working install, just one that
        asks. The action would otherwise exit non-zero under Return="check" and roll the whole
        installation back, turning "you cleared an optional setting" into "setup failed".
      -->
      <Custom Action="WriteAgentConfig" After="InstallFiles"
              Condition="NOT Installed AND APIBASEURL" />

      <!--
        Registered after the files are in place (the task names SEL.Agent.exe, which has to
        exist), and on repair as well as a fresh install: a task somebody deleted is exactly the
        thing a repair should put back.
      -->
      <Custom Action="InstallLogonTask" After="WriteAgentConfig"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE" />

      <!--
        Before RemoveFiles, while the service executable that owns the task still exists. An
        upgrade re-registers it moments later; that is harmless, because schtasks is called with
        /F and the task is identical.
      -->
      <Custom Action="RemoveLogonTask" Before="RemoveFiles"
              Condition="REMOVE~=&quot;ALL&quot;" />

      <!--
        First thing in the uninstall, before anything has been taken away, so a refusal leaves
        the machine exactly as it was rather than half-removed.

        Two conditions, each earning its place:
          REMOVE~="ALL"             only a full removal, not a feature change
          NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE  an upgrade removes the old package and must not stop to ask

        Deliberately NOT conditioned on UILevel, which was the first attempt and would have made
        the whole thing decorative: Burn runs its MSI packages with the UI level set to none, so
        an interactive "setup.exe /uninstall" — the documented route, and the one anybody would
        actually use — looks exactly like a silent removal from in here. The agent decides
        instead, because it is the only party that can tell whether a window it draws will be
        seen by a human. See App.AuthorizeUninstall.
      -->
      <Custom Action="AuthorizeUninstall" Before="InstallValidate"
              Condition="REMOVE~=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <ui:WixUI xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui" Id="WixUI_Minimal" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="License.rtf" />

  </Package>
</Wix>
